 and maybe over  time that peter's out so if you run that  for one or two weeks you might not be  able to observe that effect  i say one week because that's that's  kind of the bare minimum of when you of  how long you typically want to run an  experiment just to observe the  seasonal effects throughout the week but  um that's that could be a whole separate  talk in terms of best practices for  experiments  um but anyway so going back to  to limitations uh the second uh  limitation  is uh it's actually pretty imprecise for  measuring impact if you want to know  what would happen if we roll this out to  100  so if you remember from the previous  slide um this was  the second use case for you know why we  wouldn't want to run experiments in the  first place  um so why is that so  um if any of you have ever run  experiments in practice online  even even at a place like twitter even  when you have a lot of traffic more  often than not your results are not  statistically significant um and that  can be really hard or really frustrating  for trying to decide what to do  especially if your if your goal  is to  figure out what the what things would  look like if you roll it out to 100 so  it might be okay for fulfilling that  first use case in terms of deciding  whether or not you ship something so  it's not negative it's positive  you know it's it's not risky enough to  to not ship it but it really doesn't  help you with this if your confidence  interval is quite wide  um  so you might say well why don't we just  sum up all the experiment results and  you can't actually do that to measure  your total combined impact so  um if you were to ask most businesses or  organizations they don't  they don't really care about you know  the impact of an individual change they  want to know like what was your  cumulative  impact of like everything that we did  you did or everything that you changed  um  and it's a statistical fallacy to  actually sum everything up because there  you know  there's a lack of independence with  experiments you're running often times  there's variance in the confidence  intervals  it's not quite as simple as just summing  up the midpoint  so what's the solution uh incremental  holdouts are  uh kind of core to what i'm talking  about today and this is kind of like the  gold standard of experiment of online  controlled experiments so  um a high level overview is every  quarter or every half and i'll kind of  talk through different cadences you may  run and why you hold out a group of  users  um from all of the features your team is  launching so you're bundling all the  changes you made throughout that time  frame and holding that back from a group  of users  um  and  at the end of the quarter or end of the  half you're comparing metrics from the  holdout group to all other users and you  kind of rinse and repeat you bucket a  new fresh group of users uh into a new  holdout group next quarter and you kind  of repeat the cycle in order to  enable regular measurement of the impact  that you're making every quarter or  every half or whatever desired cadence  and why do we do this it helps us  understand the total impact of  everything we launched last quarter  which  might be a goal that a lot of  organizations would strive for  and you know it's a little more rigorous  it allows you to observe long-term  effects particularly in lagging metrics  like retention um you might not see the  results on a short-term experiment this  retention takes a while to  materialize in user behavior  um and i i guess to go back into why  it's a gold standard uh  we're statisticians right we love more  validation and it really helps you  validate  your experiment results even further so  one it helps you determine whether a  positive experiment result was due  simply to novelty effects  and two it helps you  potentially identify confounding effects  from multiple features being shipped  so something might be positive but in  conjunction with something else  it might cannibalize or wash out those  effects  and i guess i'll also say throughout  this talk when i say experiments i'm  typically referring to  these short term one to two week  experiments um or or a b tests  or a multivariate test but the key is  short term  and measuring potentially one or very  few changes and when i say hold out or  hold back i might use those  interchangeably i'm referring to this so  a bunch of changes bundled together  and  being held out from a group of users um  and  i'll go into  to this next but something that we run  into often is is this kind of uh tension  of like do we put it in hold out or do  we just run just run an experiment  um so part two is you know part part one  you can kind of see um i guess just in  summarizing holdouts and experiments you  know all this sounds pretty compelling  if you're a statistician you're like  this sounds great right that sounds so  promising why wouldn't you do this  everyone should be doing this  so  so part two is implementing a holdout  and what that looks like in practice  so i actually won't go too deep into the  engineering platform or architecture  itself today um  so you know if you're considering  running a hold out all of this assumes  that you probably already have a pretty  robust experimentation tool for running  experiments at scale  and what does that look like so on a  high level a holdout is really just a  collection of feature switch  configurations running together  for a longer period of time  so the the systems are mechanisms for  withholding features or  whether you're running an experiment  that's short term or a holdout which  runs for longer and  it's it's really the same so um i have  this meme here where you know data  scientists  we think that functionally right a b  tests and holdouts are very different um  but if you were to ask the software  engineer  they would probably say it's the same  thing  to them right it's it's maybe more work  but it's it's the same same ask  um so here's here's what a sample  holdout schedule might look like so in  in practice here's um when you implement  it it looks something like this and this  is actually an example from hulu  i thought this was a cool public blog  from them about implementing holdouts at  who and disney streaming um i  credited down there so you can take a  look  but this is what it would look like if  you were to run this in a quarterly  period  here  is a different example where you might  run it by half or six month cadence  and i'll talk more about you know  when you might decide to run at various  cadences but  you have so here you have a holdback  that's running for or hold out that's  running for for 12 months um and there's  a warm-up period and during the warm-up  period  we're bucketing or we're enrolling users  into the holdout we're observing their  behavior but we're not reporting our  results yet um  or you know the results are baking some  pms we'll say um the reporting period is  when we're actually able to evaluate and  report on the results and you're kind of  cycling through this  um over time so  all right so uh i mentioned choosing an  appropriate time frame so really what  you want to decide is how long to run  your hold out for so you don't want it  to be  so long that it becomes too costly to  maintain um and and negative to user  experience so you don't want to be  holding holding out features  or putting users in a version of your  application from like two years ago  um but not so short that you can't  observe the long-term impact because  really that's what you're trying to do  um understand the long-term impact on  metrics  um so how does this work so each holdout  runs for 12 months it's split into two  halves the first six months is your  warm-up period and the second six months  is a reporting period um so at any given  time  each engineering or product team is  maintaining both holdouts in parallel  one in warm warm-up and one in reporting  um and you'll you'll run these for 12  months total and at the end of 12 months  it's important that you retire the hold  out and release users to the production  experience just to not keep them in this  really old version of whatever you're  used to run which might be slower or  be missing a lot of desirable features  or whatnot  so lesson certain so i kind of went over  a high level overview  if you've done this maybe you're like  this is hard this seems costly if you  haven't done this or you're new to this  it might seem a little simple  um but the biggest lesson that i learned  is actually uh and this is actually a  reference also in that hula article and  um actually by a lot of  there is this paper from an online  controlled experiment summit um  where they got a lot of different  companies together and one of the  biggest findings is  um you know a lot of the hard part is  not the data science it's it's the  engineering so  um  this option running a holdout usually  has a really um  expensive engineering cost so  your development team is maintaining a  code fork um that's not updated for a  really long time and that gets really  messy um so as a data scientist i'm i'm  not the person maintaining multiple code  paths um for a year old version of of  the app i'm not dealing with the the  tech debt i might see it tangentially  but i'm not directly doing it oftentimes  so i don't directly see or work on the  investment that goes into this by  development teams and that was something  that you hear a lot from engineering  teams  um  so  what do we do about this i think the  things that you need to build um are  again focusing on your experimentation  platform and making that better um so  some of the pain points and solutions  that we identified are number one it  sometimes takes a lot of time or effort  to implement experiments so you really  want to reduce the amount of time or  effort it takes  to  um actually setting that up um two you  want to make it easier uh i guess it's  hard to ever make it easy but you want  to make it easier to maintain multiple  code paths because again you need to  support both the old and new version of  the feature for a pretty long time  um and three you want some level of  automation so  something that you hear from from teams  doing this for the first time is there's  a lot of manual or repetitive work and  anytime you do that that can introduce  bugs or mistakes  and when you're adding these feature  switches and logic  constantly to multiple holdouts it can  cause a lot of  of issues that you might not anticipate  so you want some level of automation  there hopefully  and it takes a while to build that  pattern and behavior so you can figure  out what to automate  again that could be a whole other talk  so i won't go into too much into that  today but um  there's so many other things i could  have covered but i guess one other thing  is app latency can also become an issue  so if you're  a lot of the code is invisible but it's  still there so it can boggle down your  app it can still cause a negative user  experience and it's a lot of bloat  so lesson two  getting people to follow a new process  is really really challenging  so i say this about a lot of things but  a lot of problems are actually people  and process problems  and not you know not just technical  problems so as the saying goes it's like  hurting cats  i have a lot of cats  if you also have cats you know what i  mean by this  so when you have an organization that's  like thousands of people  it becomes infinitely harder to scale  knowledge and to get teams to follow a  new protocol  so how do we make this better so  not having organizational buy-in is a  non-starter so i have that as  the first thing you really need to do  so you want to understand as with most  data requests it's you know why are we  even doing this so it's expensive to  build this what are we using it for so  some organizations might evaluate teams  based on their collective performance  using these holdouts and that might  provide some incentive  from leadership or from top down to  actually  maintain this or to actually work on  this  some organizations make it really clear  that rigorous learning is a priority  and that you want to validate your  experiment results a little bit further  to make sure people are  you know choosing experiment results by  running a lot of things i mentioned  growth teams or growth hacking right if  you try to sum up your experiment  results you might be over exaggerating  what the impact is um  so but again your your  organization needs to make it clear that  you know they see the value in this and  make it clear to teams that they should  be working on this and and why  the second thing you need is  hopefully teams are a little more mature  and well oiled and already following  some shared set of principles or  processes so that  step three when you try to adopt a  feature launch protocol  with specific guidelines on how to  actually configure features until hold  out you want to make your engineering  requirements really clear  it doesn't feel so foreign right they  already have assured language your  protocols this is just another thing  similarly you want to maintain  documentation on what features are  included in the holdout  and you want to do this for context and  for visibility when someone's trying to  interpret what's going on  a holdout again if you remember is a  number of changes together at once so  if you're not documenting that it might  be hard to remember or interpret what's  going on there  and lastly you want to develop a shared  framework for analysis and reporting um  to make sure that you're all kind of  consistently looking at this the same  way  um lesson three um  i guess one of the things that i i  arrived at i was also you know maybe  maybe you don't need a hold out and i'll  put yeah because i think you know any  any company would benefit from it if you  can do it um but one of the lessons that  i learned is learning from a few  well-run experiments are significantly  more valuable than trying to analyze a  poorly maintained holdout so  also as a reminder even even a  well-maintained holdout right doesn't  give you a deep understanding  that you would gain from running  independent experiments because there's  you don't know which specific change is  positive or negative so i would argue  that you know experiments are are more  valuable than than holdouts um and of  course the gold standard is having both  in conjunction but if i were to choose  one um you know don't put the cart  before the horse focus on getting  experiments right first um  so here are some signs a team might not  be ready to implement a holdout um so i  mentioned in lesson one if you have  engineering bandwidth  maybe there are faster cheaper ways to  get to the measurement that you need  maybe you don't need to chase precision  and kind of go down this rabbit hole  which can be costly  and again garbage can garbage out you  know if  you're not maintaining the the integrity  or  quality of your hold out it's it's not  that useful  um a second sign might be your engineers  or your development team is  inexperienced with experiments to begin  with so  um  if they have a hard time running just a  standard a b test  you want to get them familiar with  running and maintaining shorter smaller  experiments to start and getting that  right before they they graduate to  running holdouts  and lastly  one thing that ties back to that  schedule is maybe your teams aren't  shipping fast enough or at a regular  enough cadence so again developing those  patterns to require a hold out so um  if you're you're not doing that then  then you know you're probably better off  running um experiments as needed or  until you develop some sort of  regularity um same can be said for  impacting the same set of metrics  consistently um if all of your  experiments are impacting different  metrics if you roll them all into a  holdout you're probably not going to  see much value from that  so i talked about all these lessons  learned but would i do it again yes  it's great when it works so i think for  more mature teams who have all  ingredients to actually implement and  maintain a holdout um  it does what it promises so  um you you know in this new cycle you  run a standard experiment for two for  one or two weeks as you previously would  um  then you would uh if it's positive  launch the tree launch it to all users  but withhold the change from the holdout  and as promised it gives you more time  to observe the impact on lagging metrics  like retention  you ideally get past that novelty window  or novelty effect  that you see with shorter term  experiments and understand the true  impact  and it provides additional validation  to help you  actually understand compounding or  interaction effects from multiple  launches  and again this this diagram from a blog  post which is is pretty good for for  looking at another example at hulu and  disney  um so i guess to return back to to the  original  thesis or impetus for this talk  or interaction i had with my team  you know while holy having a hold out  might be the gold standard of of online  experimentation they're not always  necessary they're not always possible um  when you're trying to build causal  measurement and actually i i am pretty  impressed by some teams that run  experiments because experimentation as a  whole is really hard and just because  you have this tool or method  at your disposal it doesn't mean you  should always use it  so here are some additional resources i  i reference a few of these um  ronnie kojavi has a lot of really good  literature on practical considerations  for online controlled experiments  i cite this book quite a lot  trustworthy online controlled  experiments  is probably the best resource i've seen  for industry examples  i mentioned this blog post from from who  and sean taylor's talk  he gave one  pretty recently about experimentation  and causal measurement um that was  pretty useful as well  and  yeah that's that's all for me i hope  this was useful and  i'm a little over time but thank you and  see you all soon  all right thank you
 

